import{_ as n,o as a,c as s,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.DbIb1qZn.js";const u=JSON.parse(`{"title":"What's New","description":"A plain-language summary of what each Frensense version added.","frontmatter":{"title":"What's New","description":"A plain-language summary of what each Frensense version added."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"whats-new.md","filePath":"whats-new.md"}`),t={name:"whats-new.md"};function r(i,e,d,l,c,h){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="what-s-new-in-frensense" tabindex="-1">What&#39;s New in Frensense <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-s-new-in-frensense" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What&#39;s New in Frensense&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A quick look at what changed in each release, written for everyone (no compiler knowledge required).</p><hr><h2 id="_0-8-2-honest-coverage" tabindex="-1">0.8.2 - Honest Coverage <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0-8-2-honest-coverage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;0.8.2 - Honest Coverage&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>&quot;Clean&quot; never means &quot;not analyzed&quot;: Frensense no longer reports clean when it couldn&#39;t scan a codebase.</strong></p><p>Every scanner has two silent failures: it can find nothing because the code is safe, or because it never looked. Frensense used to print the same words (&quot;Status: Clean&quot;, &quot;Analysis Complete: No findings.&quot;) for both. On the OWASP Benchmark for Python, that meant calling a 1,230-file tree complete while 622 files contained nothing the engine models and 19 dangerous calls were left undecided.</p><p>Starting with 0.8.2, a scan has to show its work:</p><ul><li><strong>The summary shows the real ratio.</strong> <code>Files Scanned: 20/400</code> counts every source file the scan found, including files in languages Frensense cannot read (Ruby, PHP, Java and the rest). A second line names how many were skipped and why, and <code>Sink Sites</code> / <code>Not Recognized</code> / <code>Undecided</code> say how many dangerous operations were actually inspected and how many the analysis could not resolve. <code>Status: Clean</code> is reserved for a scan that read everything it found and decided every site; otherwise the status says which part is missing.</li><li><strong><code>--explain</code> lists the files you did not get.</strong> Alongside the existing &quot;why the engine stayed quiet&quot; records, it prints every unsupported language, the files that failed to parse, the files where nothing was recognized, and the exact sink sites that ended undecided.</li><li><strong>AI agents get the same answer.</strong> The MCP tools (<code>frensense_scan_file</code>, <code>frensense_audit</code>, <code>frensense_diff</code>) now return an <code>analysis</code> block with the coverage numbers, and <code>clean: true</code> means &quot;reported nothing, read every file, and decided every site it recognized&quot;, not just &quot;reported nothing&quot;. An agent that edits code and gates on <code>clean</code> is told when a file was never analyzed.</li><li><strong>CI sees it too.</strong> SARIF reports add coverage fields and a warning notification when files were not analyzed, so a pipeline cannot read a green result for a tree that was only partly scanned.</li><li><strong>JSON reports gain a few fields</strong> (<code>analysis.clean</code>, and <code>files_found</code> / <code>files_not_analyzed</code> / <code>files_unsupported</code> under <code>analysis.coverage</code>). Nothing existing was renamed or removed.</li></ul><p>This is a reporting change only: the analysis engine is untouched, and every count comes from verdicts the scan already produced.</p><hr><h2 id="_0-8-1-scan-progress" tabindex="-1">0.8.1 - Scan Progress <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0-8-1-scan-progress" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;0.8.1 - Scan Progress&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>You can now watch Frensense work.</strong></p><p>Before this release, running a scan showed the header and then went quiet until it finished. That silence made it hard to tell whether the scan was progressing or stuck.</p><p>Starting with 0.8.1:</p><ul><li><strong>In your terminal</strong>, a live progress bar updates as each file is processed. It shows the current phase (lowering files, then analyzing the program), how many files have been scanned out of the total, any errors encountered, and how much memory is in use.</li><li><strong>In CI or when piping output</strong>, clean log lines appear at regular milestones so your pipeline logs stay readable without any special terminal codes.</li><li><strong>In machine-output modes</strong> (<code>--json</code>, <code>--sarif</code>), nothing changes: structured output goes to stdout untouched; progress goes to stderr.</li><li>Use <code>--no-progress</code> to suppress all progress output if you prefer silence.</li></ul><p>The scan summary at the end also now shows <strong>Peak Memory (RSS)</strong> and a <strong>Files Scanned</strong> count.</p><hr><h2 id="_0-8-0-package-aware-scanning-and-ai-agents" tabindex="-1">0.8.0 - Package-Aware Scanning and AI Agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0-8-0-package-aware-scanning-and-ai-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;0.8.0 - Package-Aware Scanning and AI Agents&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Fewer false positives. Built-in AI agent support.</strong></p><p>Frensense 0.8.0 was a major release focused on accuracy and integration:</p><ul><li><strong>Package-scoped facts</strong>: rules now understand which library a call belongs to, so a <code>.query()</code> on a harmless UI library no longer triggers the same finding as one on a database driver.</li><li><strong>MCP server</strong>: Frensense ships a built-in Model Context Protocol server so AI coding agents can run security scans directly without shell commands.</li><li><strong>Diff-only mode</strong>: scan only the files changed in a pull request, not the whole codebase.</li><li><strong>Baseline suppression</strong>: accept known findings and only alert on new ones.</li><li><strong>Hidden source directory scanning</strong>: <code>.vitepress</code> and similar dot-prefixed source trees are now scanned alongside regular source files.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="_0-7-0-compiler-mode-engine" tabindex="-1">0.7.0 - Compiler-Mode Engine <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0-7-0-compiler-mode-engine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;0.7.0 - Compiler-Mode Engine&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Exact taint paths across functions and files.</strong></p><p>Frensense 0.7.0 rewrote the engine to treat code like a compiler does:</p><ul><li>Every file is lowered to a program graph (IR + SSA form) before analysis.</li><li>Findings include the full source-to-sink path, not just a flagged line.</li><li>Language knowledge (sinks, sanitizers, guards) is separated from the engine and stored in teachable <code>.frc</code> bundles.</li><li>Initial language support: TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go, Rust, and C.</li></ul>',26)])])}const p=n(t,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,p as default};
